update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('c8e1214f-182c-45d9-970c-7cd03c222039'::uuid,'f77e95c762f9f0a40e73ee171fd58769','Roger won''t buy the expensive vacuum because Roger is frugal and would never forgive the spending. "A penny saved is a penny earned" is the frugal person''s motto, so the story illustrates it and the phrase is used properly.'),
('25c2f540-0a00-41b4-be44-924f3db1b5f9'::uuid,'735758e779bc487d92d996be0a35c308','Check both ingredients. The sacrifice: the couple passes over their favorite wine, which tastes better, so they give up pleasure. The reason: they think the other wine "will impress their dinner guests," which is appearances. That is the coat again: a worse experience in exchange for a better image. The "less expensive" detail is extra; the taste is the pleasure being given up.'),
('d8c733e7-9bb7-4638-88c7-0d6cdffa8ac9'::uuid,'43ca5ca7a8622caa9b5a385c6abb28f4','C is about how early in life a child meets an object and how easily the child learns not to misuse the word. The study has one group of young children and never compares ages. It also shows the children misusing the word, not learning to avoid misuse, so neither half of C has an example here.'),
('cb01788e-5b7d-4580-876b-8530c8be03aa'::uuid,'b22baecbde4242675f47c00501278f73','Match D to the study piece by piece. "learn a word by observing how the object denoted by that word is used": the children learned "stairs" while walking up and down stairs. "sometimes apply that word to a different object": they called a ladder stairs. "that is similarly used": both are things you climb. Every part of D has a matching fact, and D stays modest with "sometimes," which is all one study can illustrate.'),
('a0dabe1a-8844-44c7-b890-aaf04db0c502'::uuid,'1c249c5f5dab1f6fa20c404693baf123','A contrasts who really has the most power with who the public thinks has the most power. The case is about power people feel they have over a figure, not how powerful a figure seems to be. It never says the public misjudges who is most powerful, so A''s contrast has no example here.'),
('f1300a61-c45e-4816-b53d-112537638812'::uuid,'1387ad86ba27450163a48df80f37c640','B ties familiarity to how much power people ascribe to someone. The stimulus sets familiarity aside as not the main reason for blame, and never says familiarity changes how powerful anyone seems. B also leaves out blame, which is the behavior the whole case describes.'),
('6fd26bd8-e5d9-4c4c-b2dd-4e2cdb59ede4'::uuid,'a9d3b50a25c74c69777e6edcf517f40d','C is the tempting one, because politicians are well known and they do get blamed. But C makes being well known the reason for blame, and the stimulus says the blame is "not primarily because politicians are more familiar." The case illustrates a different cause, felt power. An answer that adopts the explanation the author rejects can''t be what the case illustrates.'),
('39743271-aedf-4cc7-ae43-350d50350193'::uuid,'646ab37c47df644419f5c82f9699c92b','D is about whether public figures are blamed enough for the problems they cause. The stimulus never judges whether the blame is the right amount; it explains why one group gets more blame than another. If anything, politicians get more blame than figures who are at least as responsible, which points away from "not held sufficiently responsible."'),
('d900629c-87dd-4d7f-acf8-b61fbcf0ea8b'::uuid,'da12530b5532dcce367b4b59f55c4913','Match the parts. "a publicly known person" covers politicians. "someone over whom they feel they have power" is the author''s own reason. "more inclined to blame" is the behavior in the case. E turns the case into a rule by swapping the specific group for a general category while keeping the cause the author endorses. Test it by plugging the case back in: people feel power over politicians, so they blame politicians more. That is the stimulus.'),
('9901c3d7-142b-4104-9488-80a979c2869e'::uuid,'3200021689453af4bbc0648392e8c99f','A ties precision to how detailed and complete the evidence is. The stimulus never describes the evidence behind the music or jazz predictions, let alone says it is more or less detailed. The only thing we see change is the target, from professors to music professors to jazz studies professors. A case that never shows the evidence varying can''t illustrate a rule about evidence.'),
('beeeaf67-2e21-4f2f-a80e-48e500bf8ab2'::uuid,'55a6d151b8bd2424891af063e8db8489','Line up the three predictions. All professors: reasonably accurate. Music professors, a narrower group: less accurate. Jazz studies professors, narrower still: less accurate again. Each step down makes the prediction more specific and less accurate, which is B read in reverse: the more general the level, the more accurate. On illustrate questions, name what changes across the case and what changes with it, then find the answer that links those two things.'),
('a1cf05f6-2b77-488b-b0ae-b5f276185dff'::uuid,'ed62da888831c469940ed2786127a109','D has A''s problem with a different outcome word: confidence instead of precision. Nothing in the stimulus says the evidence for one prediction is more detailed or complete than the evidence for another, so the case shows no example of better evidence earning more confidence. What changes is how specific the prediction is.'),
('5a34b232-1c82-4c9b-8614-148e78cabbb6'::uuid,'1c80e9d823b43d3c265950c3b892d41c','"It" refers back to efficiency, so A says some measures that seem to boost efficiency actually hurt efficiency. The stimulus says the opposite about Taylorism: it produced "greater efficiency" and increased productivity. The loss in the case is to the work''s intrinsic worth, not to efficiency.'),
('c094dc25-7da8-4c72-a463-d554e2589006'::uuid,'dfb8971d233da901043863ed15b92cc0','Match each piece. "Some developments in industry" is Taylorism. "benefit workers financially" is the rise in standards of living from increased productivity. "less rewarding in other respects" is the loss of intrinsic worth when the jobs became routine and repetitive. B keeps both sides of the tradeoff and stays modest with "Some" and "can," which is exactly what one case can illustrate.'),
('36de212e-6eda-4dcf-92fe-d243c4dca6b2'::uuid,'e2293dd2b3425df8b2097a2975ffca4d','C swaps in a different loss: workers devoting less effort to their jobs. The stimulus says the jobs became routine and repetitive, not that workers try less, and a repetitive job can take plenty of effort. The loss in the case is the work''s intrinsic worth, which C never mentions.'),
('1faf37f7-7be3-4b14-bd74-a5e68f98f1ea'::uuid,'1992591ad8a60006900759e37e002c0a','D is the closest trap because it uses both sides of the stimulus: higher living standards and something like valuing the job less. But D says a rise in living standards "will likely involve" a drop in how much workers value their jobs, as if the first brings the second. In the case, both came from Taylor''s methods; the higher living standard didn''t produce the routine work. D also stretches "some of the intrinsic worth" into how much workers value their jobs "overall," and the stimulus never says what workers themselves value.'),
('86362718-da1f-449a-a2d7-7eabf3e112b7'::uuid,'5d83334dca9eef3b51dc56c73b77ee68','E compares two kinds of workers, highly creative ones and average ones, and says the creative ones are sometimes less efficient. The stimulus compares the same jobs before and after Taylorism, not different workers. It never measures anyone''s creativity or says creative workers are slower.'),
('c52ddec7-ec2f-49ea-b2e7-2a54d5b9fff6'::uuid,'30b42c62fc8e2eb978c8737a32bfc61d','A is about people who see several ways to reduce a risk and take only the one they think works best. The journalist never mentions people choosing between safety measures. The argument is about protection leading people to take more risks, so both A''s trigger (several measures available) and its outcome (picking just one) miss.'),
('bf25f385-b25d-4d8a-bbb5-afde8af02948'::uuid,'dd28740b408db7e6c375f2ad46b6cd30','Set B beside both halves of the analogy. "ways to protect people from the consequences" is the SUV''s lower injury risk and the cancer cure. "behaviors that would normally harm them" is careless driving, smoking, and too much sun. "less careful to avoid such behaviors" is drivers driving less carefully and people smoking and tanning more. B is the Goal in general terms: protection from the downside makes the risky behavior more likely. When the reasoning is an analogy, the right principle is the one rule that covers both halves.'),
('f629f96d-2160-4046-ad61-321886f85b96'::uuid,'9d50c3c77bdb256e97737a61e54407ad','C says people take special care with behaviors they think will likely seriously injure them, and none with behaviors they think are harmless. The journalist''s people don''t think careless driving or smoking is harmless; they think the risk is lower or the disease curable. C also never says that reducing a risk makes people less careful, which is the journalist''s whole move.'),
('527e3d7c-082e-4d93-abe5-27b16a6b32fd'::uuid,'9d23112d140598ab8bd858dfd2e1efdf','D compares activities with known risks to activities of unknown risk. In the journalist''s examples the risks are known: SUV drivers "correctly" believe the risk is lower, and smoking and sun exposure are "known to increase the risks" of cancer. What changes is how bad the consequences are, not whether the risk is known, so D''s comparison never appears.'),
('6071dc4d-6710-40b2-a5cd-3119972d3f6c'::uuid,'e9e1408a5f8744dc9293053a0b6eeed8','E tempts if you reason a step ahead: a cure makes cancer a lesser harm, so other desires win out. But the journalist never sorts harms into serious and lesser and never mentions competing desires. The reasoning runs from more protection to less care. E mentions neither protection nor a change in behavior, so it doesn''t capture the link both of the journalist''s examples rely on.'),
('71b7fecc-9c8e-4bab-a960-5281b8994111'::uuid,'8b18e77a7ffae26d479183c6f86df042','A concludes the government is unjustified in interfering with Jerry''s moviemaking. Our principle can only prove that interference is justified. Jerry''s facts (no harm, a motive to benefit the children) show the triggers are missing, but a missing trigger never proves the opposite outcome. The principle simply has nothing to say about Jerry.'),
('fb983a5a-200b-456e-b16a-e28af79aa6c1'::uuid,'871aab9c62ebba0365ce53dcc9f79e8b','B has the right kind of outcome, a city council justified in acting, but the harm is aesthetic: a property is "less pleasing" to look at. The principle needs an increased likelihood of physical harm to others, and an unmowed lawn doesn''t make anyone more likely to get hurt. With the first trigger missing, the neighbor''s motive never matters.'),
('988fc8a3-804f-4bcb-9871-bdd77a802fe1'::uuid,'e0b8a2a0fb48d203f1cc49dc01520cf2','C is tempting because it involves physical injury and a government rule. But ask who gets hurt when a rider skips the helmet: the rider. The principle requires harm to others, so the first trigger fails. C also says nothing about the rider''s motive, so the second trigger is never established either.'),
('5978fe37-f39b-4f52-b14c-7d076a311670'::uuid,'28ed6cd3dc3246ec3431a9c32ba870b6','D gets the second trigger: Zabziew''s research is motivated by profit, "not by the desire to benefit customers." The first trigger fails. Being "of little help to people suffering from serious illness" is not increasing the likelihood of physical harm to them. Failing to help someone isn''t harming them, so the principle never fires.'),
('711c3689-a7e1-4279-814f-7a9c55a14617'::uuid,'cb0bf19b6f8be42face5cb89e01a6b23','Check both triggers against the facts. Physical harm to others: the speech "would most likely have caused a riot and people would have gotten hurt." Not motivated by a desire to help others: Jill''s aim was to further Jill''s own political ambitions. Both are established, so the rule delivers its outcome, and E''s conclusion matches it: the government was justified in preventing the speech. That is the move for every conform question with a conditional rule: confirm each trigger with facts in the choice, then make sure the conclusion is the rule''s right side.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;